
### Summary

The above programs implement five different sorting algorithms in Python without using built-in sorting functions: **Bubble Sort, Quick Sort, Selection Sort, Insertion Sort, and Merge Sort**. Each program accepts the number of elements and values from the user, sorts the elements, and calculates the execution time using Python's `time.perf_counter()` function. The time and space complexities of each algorithm are also displayed.

The algorithms have different performance characteristics. Bubble Sort, Selection Sort, and Insertion Sort generally require **O(n²)** time in average and worst cases, while Quick Sort and Merge Sort are more efficient for large datasets, with average **O(n log n)** time. Merge Sort provides **O(n log n)** time even in the worst case.

### Conclusion

From the comparison of these sorting algorithms, it can be concluded that **Merge Sort and Quick Sort are generally more efficient for large datasets** because of their O(n log n) average performance. However, their memory requirements and implementation details differ. Bubble Sort, Selection Sort, and Insertion Sort are simpler to understand and implement but become inefficient when the number of elements increases.

The execution-time measurement demonstrates that the actual running time depends on the input size, input arrangement, and system performance. Therefore, selecting a sorting algorithm depends on the **size of the data, required efficiency, and available memory**. Overall, this experiment helps in understanding the practical differences between common sorting algorithms and their time and space complexities.
